# .installignore — source files excluded from the install fileset.
#
# Consumed by the Python installer (packages/installer) at the staging step.
# scripts/install.sh is a thin exec stub that delegates to the Python
# installer and never reads this file itself — there is no bash grammar to
# keep in parity with.
#
# Grammar: one pattern per line; '#' comment lines and blank lines are
# ignored.
#
# - A trailing '/' marks a DIRECTORY pattern (matched against directory names
#   only); without it, the pattern is a FILE pattern (matched against file
#   basenames only). Excluding a directory prunes its whole subtree — nothing
#   beneath it needs its own entry.
# - A leading '/' ANCHORS the pattern to the direct children of a staged
#   namespace subdirectory (skills/, rules/, agents/, commands/, hooks/).
#   Without a leading '/', the pattern matches at ANY depth, including inside
#   a DIR item's own interior (e.g. a skill's nested scripts/ subdir) — depth
#   an anchored pattern can never reach, since a skill directory stages as one
#   DIR item and its contents are never walked against an anchored entry.
# - '*', '?' and '[...]' make the pattern a glob, matched with
#   fnmatch.fnmatchcase against a single path component (case-sensitive). A
#   name with none of those characters matches exactly.
# - A '/' anywhere other than the leading or trailing position is a parse
#   error. No '**', no negation.
#
# Anchoring is explicit, not inferred from glob-ness: if unanchored were the
# only mode, /README.md would reach arbitrary depth and a skill that later
# ships references/README.md would silently stop deploying it.
#
# The real tool-root instruction files are *.md.template and are NOT matched
# by these bare basenames. Live leakers today: AGENTS.md as a direct child of
# rules/ and of skills/, in both the shared and the Claude trees -- three files,
# not one. CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md and README.md match nothing at namespace level
# today and are class coverage (defensive; see the design spec's audit table).
/AGENTS.md
/CLAUDE.md
/GEMINI.md
/README.md
# Defensive directory coverage. No rules-readmes/ directory exists today; when
# one is added it will sit at a tool root (sibling of rules/), which is never
# staged. This entry guards the hypothetical namespace-level child instead.
/rules-readmes/
# Confirmed live leaker, not defensive coverage — but only because THIS ONE
# sits at the depth an anchored pattern reaches (ruff-postedit_test.sh is a
# direct child of the hooks/ namespace dir).
/ruff-postedit_test.sh
# Dev-only artifacts that must never deploy, at ANY depth — including inside a
# DIR item's own interior (a skill's scripts/ subdir, most visibly). The
# Python patterns mirror the repo gate's suite discovery (*_test.py or
# test_*.py) deliberately: a file the gate runs as a repo-side suite must not
# be a file the installer ships. Widening one without the other lets a suite
# be both executed here and deployed downstream.
*_test.py
test_*.py
*_test.js
*.pyc
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
